# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Environment variables — Agent Farmer / Jaivik Sathi
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` and `.env.admin.local` are gitignored — never commit real keys.
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are exposed to the browser; everything else is
# server-only. Keep the service-role key and admin tokens secret.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Supabase (database + auth) ──
# Project URL and public anon key — safe to expose to the client.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Service-role key — SERVER ONLY. Full database access, bypasses RLS. Keep secret.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# ── Bot protection (Cloudflare Turnstile) ──
# Optional but recommended for a public deploy: protects the auth forms and the
# landing feedback form. Free keys at https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/turnstile
# The site key is public; the secret key is server-only. Also enable Turnstile in
# the Supabase dashboard (Auth → Bot & Abuse Protection). See SECURITY.md.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Groq (LLM) ──
# Powers the AI assistant, disease advisory, and market/scheme advice.
# Get one at https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=your-groq-api-key

# Text model for the assistant, crop recommendations, schemes, market advice and
# the disease narrative. Optional — defaults to openai/gpt-oss-120b.
#
# Groq retires models without notice, and when it happens EVERY AI feature fails
# at once with a 404. If that happens, check https://console.groq.com/docs/models
# and set a current id here — no code change or redeploy of the app is needed.
# Avoid openai/gpt-oss-20b (returns empty content, answers via a separate
# reasoning field) and qwen/qwen3.6-27b (leaks <think> blocks into replies).
GROQ_TEXT_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b

# Multimodal model for the disease-scanner vision fallback. Optional — defaults to
# qwen/qwen3.6-27b. Groq retires vision models fairly often; if scans start failing
# with a "model_not_found" error, check https://console.groq.com/docs/models and set
# a current multimodal id here rather than editing the code.
GROQ_VISION_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.6-27b

# ── News feed ──
# Powers /dashboard/news. All three are optional; the route tries them in order
# GNews → NewsAPI → Groq, and without any of them the feed shows nothing.
#
# GNews.io — the production provider. Free tier (100 req/day, 10 articles each)
# works from a deployed origin. Get one at https://gnews.io/register
GNEWS_API_KEY=your-gnews-key
#
# NewsAPI.org — secondary. Its free plan ONLY serves localhost (401s from a
# deployed domain), so it is dev-only unless you pay. https://newsapi.org/register
NEWS_API_KEY=your-newsapi-key
#
# If neither is set (or both return too little), the feed falls back to
# Groq-generated news, clearly labelled "AI Generated" in the UI, via GROQ_API_KEY.

# ── Sensor readings (ThingSpeak / ESP32) ──
# Live temperature, humidity and soil-moisture from field sensors, shown on the
# dashboard directly under the weather card. All optional — with nothing set,
# every account falls back to the shared demo channel baked into
# src/app/api/sensors/route.ts (SHARED_DEMO_CHANNEL). Set these to point the
# panel at your own channel instead.
#
# Each ESP32 node publishes to a ThingSpeak channel (https://thingspeak.com).
# Set the channel id + its Read API Key (Channel → API Keys tab; leave the key
# blank for a public channel), then map which field number (1–8) carries each
# reading. Leave a FIELD blank if that node doesn't measure it.
#
# Node A — e.g. a DHT sensor reporting air temperature + humidity.
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_A_ID=
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_A_READ_KEY=
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_A_FIELD_TEMPERATURE=1
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_A_FIELD_HUMIDITY=2
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_A_FIELD_SOIL_MOISTURE=3
# No NPK probe is wired up, so the panel shows assumed sufficiency levels and
# says so. Map a real field here and it switches to live readings.
# THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_A_FIELD_NPK=4
#
# Node B — optional second node (e.g. a dedicated soil-moisture probe on its own
# channel). Omit entirely if one node already covers all three readings. When a
# metric is mapped by both nodes, Node A wins.
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_B_ID=
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_B_READ_KEY=
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_B_FIELD_TEMPERATURE=
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_B_FIELD_HUMIDITY=
THINGSPEAK_CHANNEL_B_FIELD_SOIL_MOISTURE=

# ── Market prices (data.gov.in Agmarknet) ──
# Real daily mandi prices for the Market page & ticker. Without it, the app
# falls back to clearly-labelled "estimated" prices, so this is optional.
# Get a free key at https://data.gov.in/ (register → "My Account" → API key).
DATA_GOV_API_KEY=your-data-gov-in-api-key

# ── Feedback notifications (Resend) ──
# The landing page's feedback form always stores submissions in Supabase. These
# three only control the "someone left feedback" email — without them the form
# still works and still saves, it just tells the visitor "we'll read it soon"
# instead of "the team has been notified", and leaves `notified_at` null so
# un-alerted rows are easy to find:
#     select * from feedback where notified_at is null;
#
# Free tier is 3,000 emails/month with no card. Get a key at
# https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_resend_api_key

# Where the alert goes — your own inbox.
FEEDBACK_NOTIFY_TO=you@gmail.com

# Optional. Defaults to Resend's shared sender, which needs no DNS setup.
# Point this at your own verified domain once you have one.
FEEDBACK_NOTIFY_FROM=Agent Farmer <onboarding@resend.dev>

# ── Disease-detection inference server (optional) ──
# FastAPI server for the hybrid CNN image scanner (see the ml/ folder).
# Only needed if you run the image-based disease detection. Without it the scanner
# still works, but falls back to the (less precise) vision model for every scan.
INFERENCE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8008

# Speech server (Chatterbox Indic voices) — see ml/tts/. Powers the speaker icon
# under every assistant reply and full voice chat. Needs a GPU host; without it the
# app keeps working and replies are simply shown as text.
TTS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8009

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Admin only — used by scripts/db-exec.mjs for running SQL migrations.
# Put these in `.env.admin.local` (also gitignored), NOT in .env.local.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=your-supabase-personal-access-token
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=your-project-ref

# ── Hazard early-warning poller ──
# Shared secret guarding /api/cron/hazards, which ingests official NDMA SACHET
# (IMD / CWC / SDMA) warnings. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Set the SAME value as a GitHub repository secret named CRON_SECRET, alongside
# APP_URL (your deployed origin), for .github/workflows/hazard-poll.yml.
CRON_SECRET=

# ── Store locator (Google Places) ──
# Server-side ONLY — never NEXT_PUBLIC_. Restrict the key in Google Cloud to the
# Places API; restrict by API rather than by IP, since Vercel egress rotates.
# Text Search sits in the Pro tier: 5,000 free calls/month. Do NOT add
# places.rating / currentOpeningHours / websiteUri to the field mask in
# src/lib/places.ts — any one of them re-prices every call to Enterprise and
# drops the free allowance to 1,000.
# Without this key the locator falls back to OpenStreetMap, then to an honest
# empty state. It never invents shops.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# ── NVIDIA Riva Translate (machine translation) ──
# Translates the irrigation engine's generated prose (the explainable factors,
# schedule reasons and alerts) into the farmer's language at request time. The
# static UI copy is NOT translated here — that lives in src/lib/i18n/, where the
# Hindi strings were pre-generated by `node scripts/translate-i18n.mjs` so they
# cost nothing to render.
#
# Optional. Without a key every locale renders the engine's English, exactly as
# it did before this was added — see TRANSLATE_LOCALES in src/lib/translate.ts.
# Get one at https://build.nvidia.com (the model is free to call there).
NVIDIA_API_KEY=your-nvidia-api-key

# Translation model. Optional — defaults to nvidia/riva-translate-4b-instruct-v2.
# The language pair is passed as a system-message tag such as `en-hi`; this is
# NOT an OpenAI-style chat model, and a plain instruction prompt makes it guess
# the target language (asking it for Hindi in prose returned Danish).
NVIDIA_TRANSLATE_MODEL=nvidia/riva-translate-4b-instruct-v2

# ── TinyFish (live cross-mandi comparison) ──
# Powers /api/live-mandi and the "Compare live prices" card on /dashboard/market.
# A web agent opens agmarknet.gov.in in a real browser and reads today's prices
# across mandis. This is not optional sugar: agmarknet.gov.in is now a
# client-side React app that serves a 1KB shell to plain HTTP clients, so the
# site cannot be read with fetch + a HTML parser at all.
#
# Optional. Without a key the card falls back to the data.gov.in Agmarknet API
# (DATA_GOV_API_KEY above) and labels itself as such, so nothing breaks.
# Create one at https://agent.tinyfish.ai/api-keys — keys are shown only once,
# and new accounts carry a small amount of free usage.
#
# Three features now share this key, through src/lib/tinyfish.ts:
#   /api/news          Search only (free) — the live-web tier that replaced a
#                      Groq fallback which used to INVENT articles.
#   /api/schemes       the full ladder — Search and Fetch find and read state
#                      scheme portals; the agent is escalated to only when a
#                      page proves unreadable (myscheme.gov.in is a SPA).
#   /api/input-prices  Search and Fetch first, agent for the storefronts that
#                      render their listings client-side.
#
# Search and Fetch are free. Only the agent bills, at $0.016 per step (~5 s of
# browser per step). Every agent-calling route is session-gated and rate
# limited, and each request carries a hard USD ceiling enforced BEFORE the run
# starts — see TfBudget in src/lib/tinyfish.ts.
#
# TINYFISH_API_KEYS is a comma-separated pool. Each key carries its own quota,
# so one variable could only ever reach a fifth of a five-key grant. Selection
# is least-spent-first, read from the tf_key_ledger table (migration 032) —
# module state cannot be used for this, because every serverless cold start
# would begin at the same key. Order matters and must stay stable: the position
# is persisted against in-flight agent runs, and a run id resolves only for the
# key that created it.
TINYFISH_API_KEYS=key-one,key-two,key-three
#
# Still honoured as a single-key fallback, so an environment that has not been
# updated keeps working rather than losing TinyFish entirely.
# TINYFISH_API_KEY=your-tinyfish-api-key

# Kill switch for the paid tier only. Set to 0 and every agent rung reports
# "disabled" while Search and Fetch keep working, so the app degrades to its
# free tiers instead of breaking. This is the button to press if the wallet
# runs low mid-demo — it needs no redeploy.
# TINYFISH_AGENT_ENABLED=0
